le":"Child Process Node.js v6.2.1-rc.1 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v6.2.1-rc.1/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v6.8.0/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v8.2.1/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/release/v8.6.0/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v4.0.0-rc.4 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v4.0.0-rc.4/docs/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process Node.js v0.11.13 Manual & Documentation","url":"https://nodejs.org/docs/v0.11.13/api/child_process.html"},{"title":"Child Process","url":"https://nodejs.org/download/rc/v6.11.4-rc.2/docs/api/child_process.html"}]},"Based on the search results, here's information about Node.js `child_process.spawnSync()` documentation:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `child_process.spawnSync()` function provides equivalent functionality to the asynchronous `spawn()` method in a synchronous manner that blocks the event loop until the spawned process either exits or is terminated.\n\n## Key Characteristics\n\nThe `spawnSync()` method is synchronous and will block the Node.js event loop, pausing execution of any additional code until the spawned process exits. Blocking calls like these are mostly useful for simplifying general purpose scripting tasks and for simplifying the loading/processing of application configuration at startup.\n\n## Basic Syntax\n\nThe function signature is `child_process.spawnSync(command[, args][, options])`, where command is a string representing the command to run, and args is an array of string arguments.\n\n## Return Value\n\nThe return value is an object containing the pid (process ID), output (array of results from stdio output), stdout and stderr (contents of output[1] and output[2]), and status (the exit code of the process).\n\n## Error Handling